A carve that checks the block's size but not where it starts
hands out a range no single NAPOT entry describes:
a power of two at an offset that is not a multiple of it.
A frame is one block word, so it comes out as the block that holds the start,
which only the carve's arguments tell from the block they name.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a carve made other than what it names of its source
Witness: fuzz carve-takes-blocks

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -151,7 +151,7 @@ static int op_frame(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
         uint32_t off = arg[1];
         uint32_t len = arg[2];
         /* A block within the frame, so that every frame capability is one NAPOT entry. */
-        if (off >= size || len > size - off || !napot_block(base + off, len)) {
+        if (off >= size || len > size - off || !napot_block(base, len)) {
             return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
         }
         struct cap sub = cap_to_frame(base + off, len, cap->rights);
